{% extends "base.html.j2" %}
{% block main %}
<section class="hero">
  <a class="pill" href="#install"><b>New</b> v{{ version }} · one-line install and Docker image ›</a>
  <p class="eyebrow">FinOps for AWS, from your terminal</p>
  <h1>See every dollar.<br><span class="grad">Act on every insight.</span></h1>
  <p class="lede">spendlens finds untagged resources, oversized instances, idle waste and unexpected cost spikes, puts a
    price on each one, and answers your questions in plain English. Read-only, by design.</p>
  <div class="ctas">
    <a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}docs/getting-started/">Get started</a>
    <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ root }}architecture/">How it works ›</a>
  </div>
  <div class="terminal" role="img" aria-label="Example terminal session running spendlens waste and spendlens ask">
    <div class="bar"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div>
<pre><span class="p">$</span> spendlens waste --summary --by service
<span class="dim">service      total  alarm  compliance  est. saving/mo</span>
ebs             48      7      85.4%          <span class="hl">$507.75</span>
rds              6      1      83.3%           <span class="hl">$49.64</span>
elb              6      2      66.7%           <span class="hl">$32.86</span>
<span class="bad">14 alarm(s)</span> on 14 resources · est. saving <span class="ok">USD 595.10/month</span>

<span class="p">$</span> spendlens ask "Why did our bill go up last month?"
<span class="dim">› history_query · 2 queries · read-only</span>
Spend rose <span class="hl">+$959 (+1.8%)</span> to $55.4K in October. EC2 grew <span class="hl">+$3,698</span> and
Amazon Bedrock is new (<span class="hl">+$1,660</span>), partly offset by Redshift being
decommissioned (<span class="ok">−$3,710</span>). Source: cost_diff run #7, 2026-11-09.</pre>
  </div>
  <p class="terminal-note">Sample output from the bundled demo data (<code>--source demo</code>).</p>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat"><b>{{ stats.controls }}</b><span>built-in controls</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{{ stats.resource_types }}</b><span>taggable resource types</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>{{ stats.providers }}</b><span>AI providers</span></div>
      <div class="stat"><b>0</b><span>writes to your AWS account</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="features">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Everything FinOps needs.<br>In one command line.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Every check runs against your real AWS estate through Steampipe, with your company's own standards
      from a single <code>finops.yaml</code>.</p>
    <div class="grid">
      {% for f in features %}
      <article class="card">
        <div class="icon" aria-hidden="true">{{ f.icon|safe }}</div>
        <h3>{{ f.title }}</h3>
        <p>{{ f.body }}</p>
        <p><code>{{ f.cmd }}</code></p>
        <a class="more" href="{{ root }}docs/{{ f.doc }}/">Learn more ›</a>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="screenshots">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">See it in action.</h2>
    <p class="sub">A dashboard you can share, and a terminal that answers the question in front of you. Every screenshot
      below comes from the bundled demo data, so you can reproduce it with <code>--source demo</code>.</p>
    <div class="gallery">
      <div class="seg" role="tablist" aria-label="Screenshots">
        {% for s in screens %}<button type="button" role="tab" id="{{ s.id }}-tab" aria-controls="{{ s.id }}"
          aria-selected="{{ 'true' if loop.first else 'false' }}" tabindex="{{ 0 if loop.first else -1 }}">{{ s.tab }}</button>{% endfor %}
      </div>
      {% for s in screens %}
      <figure class="shot" id="{{ s.id }}" role="tabpanel" aria-labelledby="{{ s.id }}-tab"{% if not loop.first %} data-hidden{% endif %}>
        <a href="{{ root }}{{ s.src }}" class="frame" aria-label="Open full-size screenshot: {{ s.title }}">
          <img src="{{ root }}{{ s.src }}" width="{{ s.w }}" height="{{ s.h }}" alt="{{ s.title }}: {{ s.caption }}"
               loading="{{ 'eager' if loop.first else 'lazy' }}" decoding="async"></a>
        <figcaption><b>{{ s.title }}.</b> {{ s.caption }}</figcaption>
      </figure>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section" id="use-cases">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Built for the questions<br>teams actually ask.</h2>
    <p class="sub">From the weekly platform review to the month-end forecast, spendlens fits how engineering and finance
      already work.</p>
    <div class="cases">
      {% for c in cases %}
      <article class="case">
        <div class="who">{{ c.who }}</div>
        <h3>{{ c.title }}</h3>
        <p>{{ c.body }}</p>
        <pre><code>{{ c.cmd }}</code></pre>
      </article>
      {% endfor %}
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2 class="h2">Up and running in minutes.</h2>
    <p class="sub">Uses whatever AWS role you already have assumed. No agents, no SaaS, no stored keys.</p>
    <div class="steps two" id="install">
      <div class="step wide"><h3>Install</h3>
        <div class="install-grid">
          <div><h4>macOS / Linux</h4><p>One command installs spendlens, Steampipe, Powerpipe and the AWS plugin. No sudo.</p>
            <pre><code>curl -fsSL {{ site_url }}/install.sh | sh</code></pre>
            <p class="small"><a href="{{ root }}docs/getting-started/">Installer options and manual install ›</a></p></div>
          <div><h4>Docker</h4><p>A ready-made image with everything included, for amd64 and arm64.</p>
            <pre><code>docker pull abhaypratap/spendlens
docker run --rm abhaypratap/spendlens tags --source demo</code></pre>
            <p class="small"><a href="{{ root }}docs/docker/">Running against your account ›</a> · <a href="https://hub.docker.com/r/abhaypratap/spendlens">Docker Hub ›</a></p></div>
        </div></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Describe your standards</h3><p>Mandatory tags, allowed instance types, budgets.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens init
spendlens doctor</code></pre></div>
      <div class="step"><h3>Run, review, schedule</h3><p>Try it on sample data first, then point it at your account.</p>
        <pre><code>spendlens report --source demo
spendlens init --ci gitlab</code></pre></div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="text-align:center">
    <h2 class="h2">Read-only. By design.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin-bottom:28px">spendlens never creates, changes, tags or deletes anything. Savings are
      proposals for a human to review. In CI it assumes a read-only role through OIDC, so no AWS keys are stored. Its AI
      assistant can only run SQL that passes a parser and then executes on connections that cannot write.</p>
    <div class="ctas"><a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}architecture/">See the architecture</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM permissions ›</a></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section alt" id="faq">
  <div class="wrap narrow">
    <h2 class="h2">Questions, answered.</h2>
    <div class="faq">
      <details><summary>Can spendlens change anything in my AWS account?</summary>
        <p>No. It only makes read API calls, through Steampipe. Findings and savings are recommendations for a person to
          review, and the <a href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM policy</a> we document grants read-only access.
          The AI assistant's SQL has to pass a parser and then runs on connections that cannot write.</p></details>
      <details><summary>What does it cost to run?</summary>
        <p>spendlens has no subscription and nothing to host. On the AWS side, Cost Explorer API requests cost $0.01
          each. spendlens bounds every query to the periods it needs (typically one request per period), so a full
          <code>spendlens report</code> costs cents. CloudWatch metric reads for rightsizing fall under normal CloudWatch API pricing, which includes a free
          tier. If you use the AI assistant, your LLM provider bills you as usual.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does my data leave my machine?</summary>
        <div class="a"><p>spendlens itself sends no telemetry. Data goes only where you send it:</p>
          <ul>
            <li>AWS, for the read calls;</li>
            <li>the LLM provider you configure, and only when you run <code>ask</code>;</li>
            <li>Slack or Teams, and only when you run <code>notify</code>.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>With Amazon Bedrock, the assistant's requests go to Bedrock under your own AWS account.</p></div></details>
      <details><summary>Which permissions does it need?</summary>
        <p>A read-only role. The <a href="{{ root }}docs/permissions/">IAM permissions</a> page lists every action
          by feature, so you can grant only what you use. In CI it assumes that role through OIDC, so no access keys are
          stored.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Does it work across multiple AWS accounts?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Point Steampipe at an aggregator connection that spans your accounts and set
          <code>steampipe.connection</code> in <code>finops.yaml</code>. Findings carry their account ID, and
          <code>cost diff -g account</code> breaks spend down by account.</p></details>
      <details><summary>How accurate are the savings estimates?</summary>
        <p>spendlens's own estimates come from unit prices in <code>finops.yaml</code>. The defaults are us-east-1
          list prices; override them for your region or discounts. Use the estimates to rank work, then review. AWS
          Cost Optimization Hub's usage-based figures take priority wherever both exist, and
          <code>savings summary</code> counts each resource once.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I run it on Windows?</summary>
        <p>Yes, with the <a href="{{ root }}docs/docker/">Docker image</a>
          (<code>docker pull abhaypratap/spendlens</code>) or inside WSL. The one-line installer supports macOS and
          Linux.</p></details>
      <details><summary>Can I add my own checks?</summary>
        <p>Yes. Rules are SQL controls in a Powerpipe mod, and thresholds are parameters. Plugins can add controls,
          commands, savings estimators, AI tools and dashboard tabs without forking. See
          <a href="{{ root }}docs/extending/">Extending</a>.</p></details>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="wrap narrow" style="text-align:center">
    <h2 class="h2">Find your first saving<br>in five minutes.</h2>
    <p class="sub" style="margin-bottom:24px">Try it on sample data first; nothing to connect.</p>
    <pre class="cta-code"><code>curl -fsSL {{ site_url }}/install.sh | sh
spendlens report --source demo --out demo-report</code></pre>
    <div class="ctas"><a class="btn primary" href="{{ root }}docs/getting-started/">Read the guide</a>
      <a class="btn ghost" href="{{ repo_url }}">View on GitHub ›</a></div>
  </div>
</section>
{% endblock %}
